4. Imagine you are helping a friend who only uses GUI apps like Spotify and Zomato on their phone. Write a short guide (5-6 lines) explaining why learning the Linux CLI can be powerful for tasks like installing software, managing files, or troubleshooting issues.
=>The CLI (Command Line Interface) lets you do in one line what might take five clicks in a GUI — like installing an app with sudo apt install spotify-client instead of hunting through an app store. It's also the only way to fix things when the GUI itself breaks or won't load. CLI commands can be automated and repeated (scripts), so tasks like backing up files or updating software happen instantly instead of manually every time. You get precise control — moving, renaming, or searching thousands of files with one command instead of dragging them one by one. And when troubleshooting, error messages in the terminal actually tell you what's wrong, whereas GUIs often just show a vague popup. In short: GUI is great for casual use, but CLI is where real control and speed live.
